    "question": {
      ":question/id": "77889b16-12fd-5c0f-9ec9-aa3498b12734",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,-2)$,$(-3,-2)$,$(2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,-2)}=3\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(3)}=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-2)}=3\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(-3)}=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=3\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(2)}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/visual-repair/assets/8e411d9e1d3b79301515e36c16faa4505d6b393381238b8fa3ecac8960f81184.png)",
